  <h2>MultiWays interplay</h2>

  <p>
    On a MultiWays set the number of visible cells per reel can change
    between spins, and that interacts with cascades in one specific way:
    shape changes apply at the start of a spin (ADR 015), never in the
    middle of a chain. <code>setShape(cellsPerReel)</code> records the target
    shape, emits <code>shape:changed</code> and migrates pins straight away.
    It throws if the set was not built with <code>.multiways()</code>, if it
    is called after <code>setResult()</code> in the current spin, or if a
    value is outside <code>[minCells, maxCells]</code>.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    Call <code>setShape()</code> before <code>setResult()</code>, or the
    engine throws. For a cascade spin, call it before <code>spin()</code> as
    well: the adjust then runs before the fall, so the reel drops at its
    target height rather than reshaping afterwards. A reshape mid-chain
    inside <code>runCascade</code> is not supported; the chain uses the
    shape the spin landed with, and it reads per-reel cell counts so it
    handles a jagged board correctly.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Big symbols plus MultiWays still throw at build. If you read ADR 015,
    its phase names (<code>DropStartPhase</code>, <code>visibleRows</code>)
    are the old ones.
  </p>

  <h2>CascadeMode is legacy</h2>

  <p>
    You may find <code>spin/modes/CascadeMode.ts</code>, a <code>SpinningMode</code> named <code>'cascade'</code> with a gravity
    factor of 1.5 whose <code>computeDelta</code> caps the per-frame step at
    one slot pitch. It is exported, but nothing inside the engine
    instantiates it. The tumble pipeline is the three phases above, and <code>CascadeFallPhase</code> sets <code>reel.spinningMode</code> only to
    satisfy the start-phase contract.
  </p>

